        "original_id": "bac0792b-94fc-5d89-8492-e63489347573",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: High Tech Committee of the North Dallas Chamber of Commerce, Texas Instruments\n- Awards: Kilby International Awards, Nobel Prize in Physics\n- People: Jack Kilby, Robert Noyce\n- Concepts: integrated circuit\n\nFacts:\n- In 1958, Jack Kilby worked at Texas Instruments and took part with Robert Noyce in the realization of the first integrated circuit.\n- In 1990, the High Tech Committee of the North Dallas Chamber of Commerce created the Kilby International Awards to boost interest in the area.\n- The Kilby International Awards was named after inventor Jack Kilby.\n- On December 10, 2000, Jack Kilby was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics.\n- Jack St. Clair Kilby was an American electrical engineer who lived from November 8, 1923 to June 20, 2005.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "41ede1c3-5cb5-5e95-80a1-841e85cb4e2a",
        "text": "Page: Kilby International Awards. Sentence: The Kilby International Awards was an award created by the High Tech Committee of the North Dallas Chamber of Commerce, in 1990 to boost interest in the area.\nPage: Kilby International Awards. Sentence: It was named after inventor Jack Kilby.\nPage: Jack Kilby. Sentence: Jack St. Clair Kilby (November 8, 1923 – June 20, 2005) was an American electrical engineer who took part (along with Robert Noyce) in the realization of the first integrated circuit while working at Texas Instruments (TI) in 1958.\nPage: Jack Kilby. Sentence: He was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics on December 10, 2000.",
